bodyText:
By statute, every North Carolina residential tenant must:
- keep the part of the premises the tenant occupies and uses as clean and safe as its condition permits and cause no unsafe or unsanitary conditions in common areas the tenant uses;
- dispose of ashes, rubbish, garbage and other waste cleanly and safely;
- keep plumbing fixtures as clean as their condition permits;
- not deliberately or negligently damage, deface or remove any part of the premises, disable the smoke or carbon monoxide alarm, or knowingly let anyone else do so;
- comply with the obligations building and housing codes place on tenants;
- tell you in writing when an alarm needs repair or replacement.

The tenant is also responsible for damage to property inside a unit in the tenant's exclusive control unless it came from ordinary wear and tear, your acts or your agent's, defective products or repairs you supplied or authorized, third parties who were not the tenant's invitees, or natural forces, and replaces alarm batteries during the tenancy unless you agree otherwise in writing or, for a smoke alarm, it is a tamper-resistant 10-year lithium-battery smoke alarm.

You must notify the tenant in writing of any breach of these duties except in an emergency. The tenant's duty to pay rent and to meet these duties and your duty to keep the premises fit depend on each other, the duties are enforceable by a civil action, a breach is not negligence by itself, and a tenant may not withhold rent without a court ruling. These duties apply to residential rental agreements generally, but not to vacation rentals or a dwelling furnished without any charge or rent.
